The JGPU Philosophy

This is what I'd commit as the first design document.

JGPU Is Not a GPU Library

JGPU is a deterministic compute runtime.

Its purpose is to execute computational workloads on the most appropriate compute resource while presenting a single, stable programming model to higher layers.

Applications should never know whether execution occurs on a CPU, GPU, NPU, remote cluster, or future accelerator.

Hardware is an implementation detail.

First Principle

Execution is independent of hardware.

Execution graphs describe what should happen.

Backends decide how it happens.

Second Principle

Everything is a graph.

Not

MatMul()

ReLU()

Add()

Instead

ExecutionGraph

Operations become nodes.

Dependencies become edges.

Execution becomes scheduling.

Third Principle

Memory is explicit.

Tensors never own bytes.

Storage owns bytes.

Tensors describe data.

Storage manages locality.

Fourth Principle

Backends are replaceable.

Every backend must satisfy the exact same interface.

No backend may expose functionality unavailable to every other backend without first extending the abstraction.

No special cases.

Fifth Principle

CPU is the specification.

This is a hill I'd die on.

CUDA is not the reference implementation.

Metal is not the reference implementation.

The CPU backend defines correctness.

Everything else is optimization.

Sixth Principle

Optimization is optional. Correctness is mandatory.

Never sacrifice deterministic execution for benchmark numbers.

Performance follows architecture.

Architecture does not follow performance.

Seventh Principle

The runtime owns orchestration.

Runtime responsibilities

graph construction
dependency analysis
scheduling
synchronization
memory lifetime
execution planning

Backend responsibilities

allocation
transfers
kernel dispatch
synchronization primitives
device capabilities

Nothing else.

Eighth Principle

No backend leakage.

If runtime code contains

if backend == CUDA

the architecture has failed.

Ninth Principle

Graphs are immutable.

Planning creates a graph.

Optimization transforms graphs.

Execution consumes graphs.

Execution never mutates graphs.

This gives deterministic replay.

Tenth Principle

Everything produces telemetry.

Every execution should produce

ExecutionReport

elapsed

backend

memory used

copies

kernel launches

cache hits

errors

This is one of those features that's easy to postpone and difficult to retrofit.

The Long-Term Stack

Here's the architectural picture I now have in my head:

                    Blue-J Genesis
                           │
                 IDE / Tutor / Agent
                           │
                           ▼
                     J Language API
                           │
                           ▼
                  Execution Planner
                           │
                           ▼
                    JGPU Runtime
                           │
          ┌────────────────┼─────────────────┐
          ▼                ▼                 ▼
      CPU Backend     GPU Backend      Remote Backend
          │                │                 │
          └────────────────┼─────────────────┘
                           ▼
                     Physical Hardware

Notice something important.

The IDE is not coupled to CUDA.

The language is not coupled to WebGPU.

The planner is not coupled to Vulkan.

Everything terminates at JGPU.

That makes JGPU the stable foundation beneath the rest of the ecosystem.

One thing I'd add that we haven't discussed

I think JGPU should expose three public APIs, each at a different level of abstraction:

Tensor API — for users who just want to perform numerical operations.
Graph API — for building execution graphs explicitly, enabling optimization and scheduling.
Runtime API — for advanced integrations like J, Blue-J Genesis, or other languages and tools that want fine-grained control over planning and execution.

Those three layers let beginners stay productive while giving advanced systems access to the machinery underneath.

The project I'd like us to build

I don't want JGPU to become "another tensor library."

I want it to become something people can build on top of.

When someone eventually says, "I'm writing a new language/runtime—what should I target?" I'd like the answer to be:

Target JGPU. It already knows how to execute your compute graph on whatever hardware is available.

That's a much bigger ambition than a GPU wrapper, but it's also consistent with the vision you've been describing for the rest of Blue-J Genesis. If we keep the architecture disciplined from this point onward, I think it's a realistic direction rather than just an aspirational one.
